<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kirchheim unter Teck</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kirchheim unter Teck is a town in the Esslingen district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Jura</a> below the castle of Teck. It was the main town of the small territory of the dukes of Teck until they sold it to the counts of Württemberg in the 1380s. Its castle later served as a residence for Württemberg widows and princes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kirchheim-unter-teck.jpg" alt="Kirchheim unter Teck" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Romanfi at German Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kirchheim-teck.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kirchheim unter Teck lies in the foothills of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Alb</a>, north of the Albtrauf escarpment, in the valley of the small river Lauter, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a>. The town is about 35 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> and 10 kilometres from Teck castle. The Lauter flows through the town centre, then north-west through the district of Ötlingen towards Wendlingen am Neckar, where it joins the Neckar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Traces of settlement from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, Celtic and Roman periods have been found, and Alamannic graves show three settlements in the area during the migration period. The first written mention of Kirchheim is from 960, when it came into the possession of Emperor Otto I in an exchange for the diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chur">Chur</a>, although the town certainly existed in the 6th and 7th centuries. The market and royal mint date from the mid-11th century.</p><p>The Dukes of Zähringen owned the place, and in 1186 it passed by inheritance to a lateral line, the Dukes of Teck. Between 1220 and 1230 they made it a market town under Freiburg law, Duke Louis I of Teck founded a nunnery in 1240, and Duke Conrad II began a city wall in 1270.</p><p>Economic difficulties led the Dukes of Teck to give up the town in a process that lasted from 1303 to 1386, by which Kirchheim became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and later of Württemberg.</p><p>In the 14th century the rise of the middle classes brought an economic boom, especially in the textile industry and trade. After Duke Ulrich returned from exile in 1539, Kirchheim was expanded into a Württemberg fortress and the ducal palace was built. Duke Ulrich had introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> in 1535. The town owes its present appearance to the rebuilding after a devastating fire in 1690, and in 1864 it became the end of the first private railway in Württemberg.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the population grew markedly with refugees and displaced persons, passing 20,000 in 1948, and on 1 April 1956 the town became a Große Kreisstadt. In 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> built a launch site in the Hasenholz forest for Bachem Ba 349 Natter rocket interceptors, and three concrete launch pads remain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kirchheim unter Teck belongs to the district of Esslingen and is a regional centre in the Stuttgart Region. It took in Ötlingen and Lindorf in 1935 and Nabern and Jesingen in 1974. It was long the seat of an Oberamt and was an early centre of industry: by 1911 it made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> goods, damask, pianofortes, machinery and gliders, and was the most important wool market in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Notable people connected with it include the engineer and writer Max Eyth, born there in 1836, and the glider pilot Martin Schempp, who founded Schempp-Hirth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kirchheim unter Teck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
